# =========================================================================================== #
# File: '.gitignore'
# --- [ Description ] ----------------------------------------------------------------------- #
#
# DEFAULT-DENY, EXPLICIT INCLUDES ONLY. Nothing in this working tree is tracked unless it is
# allowed below, by name. Publishing a file is therefore a deliberate act rather than an
# accident -- see docs/decision-records/org/0003-use-deny-all-gitignore-strategy.md.
#
# Entries are ROOTED with a leading '/' so an allow means one path, not every path that happens
# to end the same way. Git does not descend into an excluded directory, so every directory on
# the way to an allowed file is re-included by name; those directory lines publish nothing by
# themselves.
#
# =========================================================================================== #

# --- [ ignore everything ] ------------------------------------------------------------------ #
**

# --- [ allowed: the allowlist and normalization rules ] ------------------------------------- #
!/.gitignore
!/.gitattributes

# --- [ allowed: repository configuration ] -------------------------------------------------- #
!/pyproject.toml
!/uv.lock
!/.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc
!/.pre-commit-config.yaml

# --- [ allowed: top-level documents ] ------------------------------------------------------- #
!/LICENSE
!/README.md
!/SECURITY.md
!/CHANGELOG.md

# --- [ allowed: GitHub and editor configuration ] ------------------------------------------- #
!/.github/
!/.github/**
!/.vscode/
!/.vscode/**

# --- [ allowed: the shared quality-gate tools ] --------------------------------------------- #
# Synced from the type-template; never hand-edited here.
!/tools/
!/tools/**

# --- [ allowed: source and tests ] ---------------------------------------------------------- #
# The product modules live under src/software_update_publisher/products/<product>/. A module is
# a normal package, so adding one adds no allowlist line.
#
# Be precise about what that costs: these two entries admit any NEW file under src/ and tests/,
# not only the ones named here, which is a scoped relaxation of the exact-file rule in org
# ADR-0003. It is taken deliberately -- the alternative makes every tracked product an
# allowlist edit, which is the thing the module design exists to avoid -- and it is bounded by
# the deny rules at the end of this file, which are what actually stop an acquired artifact or
# a credential from being published.
!/src/
!/src/**
!/tests/
!/tests/**

# --- [ allowed: documentation ] ------------------------------------------------------------- #
!/docs/
!/docs/**

# --- [ never tracked, even inside an allowed directory ] ------------------------------------ #
# Build and tool output. Unrooted on purpose: these appear at any depth, including inside a
# product module.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
downloads/
/.venv/
/.mypy_cache/
/.pytest_cache/
/.ruff_cache/
/.coverage
/coverage.xml
/htmlcov/

# Acquired artifacts. This repository downloads vendor installers; none of them belong in git.
# Test fixtures for the identity readers are the deliberate exception, re-admitted below.
*.msi
*.msp
*.msix
*.msixbundle
*.appx
*.appxbundle
*.exe
*.dll
*.cab
*.msu
*.zip
*.7z
*.rar
*.gz
*.tgz
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.iso
*.img
*.bin
*.nupkg
*.jar
*.rpm
*.deb
# An installer downloaded without an extension is the normal case for several vendors, so
# staging directories are denied by name above rather than relying on a suffix.

# Anything that could carry a credential or a live account identifier.
.env
.env.*
.envrc
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
*.jks
*.keystore
*credential*
*secret*
id_rsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519

# --- [ product modules publish exactly the module template's shape ] ----------------------- #
# A suffix rule cannot catch an installer downloaded without an extension, which is the normal
# case for several vendors. So inside a product module the allowlist is by NAME: the five files
# the module template defines, plus recorded upstream responses. Anything else a module folder
# acquires stays unpublishable whatever it is called. Adding a product is still adding a
# folder, because these names are fixed by the template rather than by the product.
/src/software_update_publisher/products/*/*
!/src/software_update_publisher/products/*/__init__.py
!/src/software_update_publisher/products/*/__main__.py
!/src/software_update_publisher/products/*/product.py
!/src/software_update_publisher/products/*/README.md
!/src/software_update_publisher/products/*/fixtures/
!/src/software_update_publisher/products/*/fixtures/*.json
!/src/software_update_publisher/products/*/fixtures/*.xml
!/src/software_update_publisher/products/*/fixtures/*.txt
!/src/software_update_publisher/products/*/fixtures/*.html

# --- [ re-admitted: the identity readers need real artifacts to test against ] -------------- #
# Named exactly, under one directory, so a fixture is a deliberate act and an acquired artifact
# elsewhere in the tree still cannot be published.
!/tests/fixtures/
!/tests/fixtures/**/*.msi
!/tests/fixtures/**/*.nupkg
!/tests/fixtures/**/*.zip
!/tests/fixtures/**/*.cab
!/.env.example
